Build Specification — App 8: Pipeline Specification Wizard
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste into another conversation window. No tables, no boxes.

What It Is
An application that produces a machine-readable pipeline specification for the Null-Model Protocol. It walks the analyst through the pre-specification checklist in Appendix A of the Null-Model Protocol paper and refuses to finish until each item is stated. It takes in the analyst's answers and puts out a pipeline specification file the simulator can read. It is for anyone about to run the protocol.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Walk the analyst through the pre-specification checklist from Appendix A of the Null-Model Protocol paper.

Refuse to finish until each required item is stated.

Enforce the order of specification.

Enforce consistency between fields (for example, if selection is declared, the selection function must be specified).

Produce a machine-readable pipeline specification file in JSON.

Produce a human-readable summary of the specification.

Validate the specification against a schema.

Support saving and loading of partial specifications.

Support the analyst returning to a partial specification.

Record the analyst's answers with the timestamp and the tool version.

Warn the analyst about items that are commonly overlooked (selection effects, measurement uncertainty, dependence structure, support rule).

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same answers produce same specification.

No network dependency.

Fast: produce a specification in under one minute of interaction.

Versioned: the specification records the schema version and the checklist version.

Reproducible: the specification records all answers and the checklist version.

Input Specification
Input 1. Analyst's answers. Required. The answers are collected interactively.

Input 2. Partial specification. Optional. A previously saved partial specification to resume from.

Input 3. Schema version. Required. Selects the correct checklist version.

Input 4. Output file path. Required. Where to save the specification.

Input 5. Output format. Optional. Values: json (default), yaml, text.

Input 6. Strictness mode. Optional. Values: strict (all items required, default), advisory (items can be left as not_determinable with a justification).

Output Specification
Output 1. Pipeline specification file. JSON, YAML, or text. Conforming to the schema.

Output 2. Human-readable summary. Text. The specification in readable form.

Output 3. Completeness report. JSON. Which items are stated, which are missing, which are not_determinable.

Output 4. Warnings. Array of warning objects. Items that are commonly overlooked or inconsistent.

Output 5. Checklist version. String. The version of the checklist used.

Output 6. Tool version. String. The version of the wizard.

Output 7. Timestamp. ISO8601. When the specification was saved.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Checklist Loader
Loads the pre-specification checklist from Appendix A of the Null-Model Protocol paper. The checklist has the following items, in order:

Item 1. Scientific question and population.

Item 2. Selection effects, including whether n is fixed or random and the order of selection and measurement.

Item 3. Measurement uncertainty, in the form of a kernel, or a justification for neglecting it.

Item 4. Dependence structure and any clustering step, including algorithm, settings, and how each is reproduced in the simulation.

Item 5. Transformation, and its justification.

Item 6. Jittering scale, if Section 5.3 applies, with the rule for determining that scale.

Item 7. Support rule, whether S1, S2, or S3, including padding and any external procedure.

Item 8. Null model, including density, parameters, provenance, and the bandwidth rule if a KDE.

Item 9. Effective null after selection and measurement.

Item 10. Scan family, whether adjacent, pair, or fixed.

Item 11. g_min as a ratio of scales, together with whether it is in data or probability units.

Item 12. P_max, if the pair family is used.

Item 13. Boundary convention, whether B1 or B2.

Item 14. Empty-W convention.

Item 15. Tie-breaking rule.

Item 16. Gap-selection rule.

Item 17. Nuisance-estimation procedure, including the gap-exclusion loop, the truncated-likelihood treatment, and the iteration count.

Item 18. Tail definition, whether >= or >.

Item 19. N, the target tail probability, and the Monte Carlo uncertainty.

Item 20. Multiplicity procedure, including how multiple nulls are handled.

Module 2: Panel Renderer
Renders the checklist as a sequence of panels. Each panel corresponds to one item. The analyst fills in the panel and proceeds to the next.

Module 3: Field Validator
Validates each field as the analyst fills it in. Checks that the field is present if required. Checks that the value is in the permitted set. Checks consistency with other fields.

Module 4: Consistency Checker
Checks consistency between fields. Examples:

If selection effects are declared, the selection function must be specified.

If measurement uncertainty is declared, the measurement kernel must be specified.

If the scan family is pair, P_max must be specified.

If the scan family is fixed, the fixed windows must be specified.

If the support rule is S2, the padding rule must be specified.

If the support rule is S3, the external procedure must be specified.

If the null model is a KDE, the bandwidth rule must be specified.

If the effective null has atoms, the jittering prescription must be specified or the analysis must be flagged as not distribution-free.

Module 5: Warning Generator
Generates warnings for items that are commonly overlooked or inconsistent. Examples:

Selection effects declared but no selection function.

Measurement uncertainty declared but no kernel.

Dependence structure declared but no simulation procedure.

Support rule is S2 but no padding rule.

Null model is a KDE but no bandwidth rule.

Effective null has atoms but no jittering prescription.

N is below the recommended minimum for the target tail.

Tail definition is > but no declaration.

Multiplicity procedure not stated.

Module 6: Specification Builder
Assembles the pipeline specification file. Conforms to the schema.

Module 7: Report Formatter
Produces the human-readable summary, the completeness report, and the warnings.

Module 8: Save and Load
Saves the specification to a file. Loads a partial specification to resume from.

Module 9: Command-Line Interface
Parses arguments. Invokes Modules 1 through 8. Runs the wizard interactively or loads a partial specification and resumes.

Module 10: Graphical Interface (optional)
A window with the checklist panels. Real-time validation and warnings.

